From f8200dbc81831c2ea97f0315c8a80212f47869c8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: AlexProgrammerDE <40795980+AlexProgrammerDE@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 16:55:34 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] feat(bedrock): render custom entities and player skins

Import native Bedrock entity and skin rendering from viafabricplus-bedrock PR #9 as an independently reviewable feature patch.

Render supplied player skin geometry with native alpha-tested materials and submit base renderer name displays for named custom actors.

Share player appearance installation with local menu previews. Each preview owns a distinct identity and releases its textures and renderer mapping when closed, so pack geometry can use the same dispatcher path as in-world skins.

Preserve explicitly supplied geometry even when its identifier is the
standard humanoid alias. Retain ordered skin updates for their exact live
connection during Java configuration and install them when the play
listener appears. A supplemental resource pack can extend configuration,
so dropping those early updates loses remote skins. Closed connections
cannot install stale updates into a later world. Log unresolved active
native actor assets instead of silently rendering an incomplete model.

Measure native actor visibility relative to the camera world position.
Minecraft 26.3 passes absolute camera coordinates to shouldRender, so
treating them as offsets hides every custom actor in lobbies far from the
world origin. The recorded CubeCraft lobby exercises this case with the
actual native renderer, independently of protocol model evaluation.

Use the entity render state's outline color for custom actor submissions.
Minecraft 26.3's seven-argument submitModel overload supplies white tint
itself and forwards its last argument as outline color. SubmitNodeCollection
adds an outline pass for any nonzero color, so passing -1 forces opaque
white outlines independently of entity metadata or accessibility settings.

The mapped target 26.3 bytecode establishes this overload behavior. A saved
official Bedrock 1.26.51.1 Hive scene has no corresponding outlines, while
the translated scene outlines its custom actors. Use state.outlineColor,
as the native player layers already do, to preserve only requested outlines.

Carry evaluated controller materials and lighting into the native actor
renderer. Preserve ordered wildcard and exact-bone overrides by separating
visible cubes while retaining every ancestor transform. Bound render-type
caching for animated floating multipliers and send the multiplier through
DynamicTransforms instead of clipping it into an eight-bit vertex tint.

For the verified entity_alphatest and entity_alphatest_one_sided materials,
use the native strict alpha > 0.5 cutoff and preserve one-sided culling.
ignore_lighting bypasses the block/sky lightmap while keeping native ENTITY
world-normal directional shading. Bedrock 1.26.51.1 (protocol 2193) CPU
141eb6120 and installed ENTITY DXIL establish the white light input,
OverlayColor.w=0, diffuse formula, and DiscardValue.x=0.5. The licensed
Windows executable SHA-256 is
537c0aee2e79afbdc94b44b28e00f466ae62bc50e2733d953b430db9dbaa9ee7.

Validate ordered mixed-material selection and ancestor transform retention
with focused tests. Render the actual two shader variants through Mesa EGL
for 192 numerical scenarios covering world normals, alpha equality,
fractional and above-one multipliers, and lightmap bypass.

Resolve effective actor material inheritance before selecting the verified
native alpha-test shader. Apply complete definition replacement in pack
order and inherited defines/states with additions and removals. Classify
the resulting ENTITY shader fields and culling state, rather than a list
of custom material names. Unknown shader flags, states, and sampler changes
retain the existing fallback. Scope caches to the active resource pack
storage and keep material aliases on the same bounded render-type cache.

The saved CubeCraft lobby uses cc_lobby_banner:entity with ALPHA_TEST and
ignore_lighting. Bedrock 1.26.51.1 entity.material establishes that this is
the native one-sided alpha-test family. The installed material SHA-256 is
fb971460c91e30fa2071f703db699934671e92b5b62617056b68a57eb7cf3eef;
the saved server material SHA-256 is
bfdabff5a1aaae7d314966fa9ba71a244f4d6d9947300777ec1d4e3cbdcf7b61.
Private probes resolve the actual banner to cull=true. Target assets remain
private. Unverified additive and mask shader effects retain the fallback. Test inheritance, culling changes, pack replacement, invalid
shader families, cyclic parents, missing parents, and independent caches.

Retain the native FLOAT actor scale in immutable render snapshots. Apply it
around the Bedrock actor origin before Java model-origin compensation.
The saved CubeCraft protocol 2193 scene sends metadata index 38 as 1.7
for eleven banners and 5 for its hanging cube. Bedrock 1.26.51.1 renders
those larger meshes. Scale-only updates refresh the snapshot through the
core controller change result. Preserve zero and negative values without
inventing a living-entity clamp or distance multiplier.

Numeric pose tests cover origin stability and actual vertex positions at
1, 1.7, 5, 0 and -2; snapshot tests cover independent updates and copied
model lists. Native custom actors already bypass frustum bounds, while
converted item displays retain zero default width/height bounds.

Ignore valid JSON materials documents whose materials field is not an
object. A malformed neighbor must not abort the entire effective material
library after parsing. Test null, array, boolean and string fields beside
a valid inherited alpha-test definition.

Support the target ENTITY AlphaTestEmissive shader through effective
material inheritance, including entity_emissive_alpha_one_sided used by
the recorded CubeCraft hanging cube. Texture alpha is an inverse emissive
mask: alpha zero keeps colored texels fully emissive, alpha one receives
ordinary light, and fractional alpha interpolates between them. Discard
only texels whose RGBA sum is zero. Apply overlay before the light blend,
retain source alpha, and apply controller multipliers only to the lit
contribution. Keep the material's culling and opaque color target.

The installed Bedrock 1.26.51.1 Entity.material.bin SHA-256 is
403efcc7777c9712176f6715f861c873e31d8e45d0b73b8e320bd29754789058.
Unmodified Lazurite 0.11 Pass.read resolves AlphaTestEmissive at byte
1368400 with exact length-prefixed pass boundaries. Its sampled SM65
fragment shader SHA-256 is
3cd762aa42a07cb9d8557e0a4e890c3b2cdf72109673668914ce20a0795ddbdf.
DXIL establishes the RGBA discard, overlay, inverse-alpha interpolation,
source-alpha output, and final fog order. The vertex bytecode matches
ordinary AlphaTest exactly. Private Mesa EGL validation runs the actual
ported shaders through 672 lit and unlit scenarios at tolerance 1e-6,
including colored alpha-zero pixels, transparent black, fractional alpha,
overlay, world normals, and fractional or above-one light multipliers.
The separate ENTITY mask shader remains outside this verified family.

Map explicit Bedrock east and west UV faces through the same model-space
conversion as box UVs. The saved target 1.26.51.1 CubeCraft banner declares
only a west face and rotates its bone by 90 degrees plus the verified
camera query. The previous per-face mapping put that texture on the
opposite surface, so its inherited one-sided material culled the plane.
Source and Java carrier yaw are both zero in that scene. Numerical probes
show the previous triangle winding faces away from the camera and the
corrected face faces toward it without changing rotation or culling.

Semantic tests compare equivalent box and explicit lateral faces,
asymmetric surface positions, and transformed one-sided winding across
eight camera directions and four actor yaws. Native assets and probes
remain private. Client script variables remain separate, and independent legacy material defines retain their own classification.

Apply controller UV scale and offset through an immutable texture matrix
per draw. Native Bedrock 1.26.51.1 ENTITY vertex DXIL reads UVAnimation
(offset U/V, scale U/V) and computes UV times scale plus offset; the
material uniform default is (0, 0, 1, 1). Signed and zero scales remain
valid. Treat verified USE_UV_ANIM as orthogonal to the supported ordinary
and emissive alpha-test families, while retaining unsupported shader
defines as fallback material families. Numerical tests preserve existing
texture transforms and verify signed scale, offset and native defaults.

Replace Java's two-light diffuse approximation with the exact target
ENTITY world-normal polynomial for both ordinary and emissive actors:
0.45 - 0.1*nx^2 + 0.1*nz^2 + 0.35*OverlayColor.w
+ 0.275*(ny*TileLightColor.w + 1). Convert Java overlay alpha to native
OverlayColor.w as one minus its stored alpha. Native TileLightColor.w
is -1 in Nether and +1 in Overworld/End; ignore_lighting forces +1.
Retain dimension identity in immutable render types, using ChunkTracker
for native connections and the canonical Java Nether key through ViaProxy.

Native Bedrock 1.26.51.1 actor producer 141eb6120 and held-item producer
141eb7df0 read dimension flag +0x1a7. Base constructor 1423039a0 clears it;
Overworld 14c9d8490 and End 14697ba70 retain it, while Nether 14c9d6930
initialization 14c9d6cb0 sets it. ENTITY vertex SHA-256 is
6d38198816c62b18d1ed4649968cab5269e5d49af503f1d01c5278a54c56de55.

Apply ordinary overlay before all RGB lighting and preserve source alpha,
matching sampled AlphaTest fragment SHA-256
4fc97f0d5c15c6d404fe895e473440b9f0de32b5cb16a78dee677489d3222172.
The previous order let overlays bypass normal shade and the controller
multiplier. An independent actual-GLSL oracle now passes 8,064 numerical
cases at tolerance 1e-6 across material families, dimensions, world normals,
ignore_lighting, hurt overlays, inverse-alpha emission, and source alpha
independent of Java vertex, modulator and lightmap alpha. The texture UV
oracle also passes all 60 checker-texture cases. Raw native exports stay
private.

Render visible non-spectator player bodies through the shared native
alpha-test shader with lighting, overlay, and dimension state. Preserve
the inherited translucent, hidden-outline, and spectator paths. The exact
Bedrock 1.26.51.1 player client and third-person controller bind default
body geometry to entity_alphatest, with no culling and strict alpha > 0.5.
All 49 saved Hive skin resource patches specify geometry and animations
without a material override. Tests verify visibility routing and actual
opaque, depth-writing, no-cull dimension pipeline selection.

Render explicit name line breaks as independently centered styled rows.
Retain component styles and translated arguments, preserve blank row
spacing, and anchor the last row at the original name baseline. Apply
this input-based layout to names received directly or through ViaProxy
without changing single-line names, visibility, light, or depth passes.

The saved target Bedrock 1.26.51.1 protocol 2193 Hive and CubeCraft
NAME metadata contains multiline labels. Four semantic tests cover
styled siblings, inherited colors, translated arguments, blank rows,
Unicode codepoints, and baseline layout. Captures and source packs stay
private. Actual in-game label comparison remains a separate check.

Ignore only the exact USE_MASK and Blending defines in the verified legacy
actor family. Bedrock 1.26.51.1 legacy bridge 148ca7690 builds a closed
22-entry define map; neither name adds a bit. USE_COLOR_MASK is a distinct
bit14 effect and retains the fallback, as do other unverified defines,
render states, and shader families. A Blending state remains different
from the ignored define. Preserve inherited culling, emission, and UV
animation after removing these two no-op names from effect classification.

Native caller 144b0d890 selects the baseline Actor shader for the effective
ALPHA_TEST, USE_OVERLAY, and USE_UV_ANIM flags (0x280001). Its feature key
keeps Change_Color and MaskedMultitexture Off. Caller 145ee8d80 carries the
ALPHA_TEST bit independently into the draw path. The exact Actor.material.bin
SHA-256 is 8720b71a8e7b6c4d5e9c12b6a8293734919030559d1e3ca0ad452f532f3ae9e6.
Unmodified Lazurite 0.11 Pass.read resolves its AlphaTest pass at byte
2394534; baseline Fancy On SM65 fragment SHA-256 is
cb8cdc8e1f30077c97148ef36dad8b00c8491fbafb7b9d91c48b9a5b4da7e88d.
It samples one base texture without masked overwrite. Its vertex applies
normalized UV times UVAnimation.zw plus UVAnimation.xy, independently
confirmed for the actual Actor family. The saved CubeCraft galaxy texture
has alpha255 throughout, so Actor's half-alpha equality distinction does
not change its output. The erroneous fallback previously disabled its
controller UV transform and native ignore_lighting behavior. Native assets
and disassembly stay private.

Semantic tests cover no-op define inheritance, culling, emission, UV
removal, the distinct color-mask effect, unknown effects, and blending
render states. No material identifier selects this compatibility behavior.

Map explicit upper and lower UV faces through the same Y-coordinate
reflection as box UV faces. Target Bedrock 1.26.51.1, protocol 2193,
geometry parser 1460fdbf0 binds the explicit up key to native face 1 at
146101c83 and down to face 0 at 146101d2b through face parser 14610e390.
The target canonical normals at 14e7d0280 and integer steps at 14e7d6188
assign face 1 positive Y and face 0 negative Y. Inverted Java model Y
therefore requires the opposite Java face for both UV representations.
The licensed executable SHA-256 is
537c0aee2e79afbdc94b44b28e00f466ae62bc50e2733d953b430db9dbaa9ee7.

The recorded Hive logo uses one-sided zero-thickness upper/lower border
planes beneath a rotated, scaled root. The previous explicit mapping
reversed their normals and culled the intended visible upper plane.
Preserve the existing UV corner orientation independently of this surface
selection fix. Tests compare all six box/explicit surface positions and
normals, upper/lower asymmetric bounds, and zero-height winding under
parent rotation and both bone/actor scale.

Apply optional native rider anchors to the Java passenger attachment path. The licensed Bedrock 1.26.51.1 protocol 2193 Hive recording independently places two mounted player eyes at vehicle position plus vector metadata 56 rotated by negative vehicle yaw, within 1e-7 world units. The anchor includes the native player eye offset and ignores vehicle pitch. Preserve absent/invalid vector fallback and explicit zero anchors.

Publish immutable passenger/vehicle UUID bindings, vectors and eye offsets on the connection thread after metadata or resolved mount changes. Match both current Java actors before applying the snapshot, and clear it on dismount or removal. Adapt native vehicle origins to Java origins and return the passenger attachment once, allowing Java positionRider to subtract it once. This places the body, nametag and attached surfaces at the same corrected world position. Numeric tests use independently captured coordinates, sparse metadata, delayed mounts, invalid vectors and stale identities.

This path requires the add-on's native connection state. Java clients behind ViaProxy retain their ordinary attachment placement when native metadata is not exposed to the client; no approximate vehicle or server-specific offset is introduced.

Place native names at retained scaled AABB height plus .7 blocks, compensating the Java renderer .5-block padding by .2 only when height is known. Bedrock 1.26.51.1 producer 141d55a60 splits LF and cancels the internal actor Y offset against its collision bounds. Cache immutable actor identity and bounds per connection and discard them on removal. Ordinary name_tag_text is unlit; preserve depth-tested/sneaking light and actor body light. Gate multiline and unlit names on direct Bedrock or accepted converted server metadata. ViaProxy Interaction metadata supplies known non-default heights; preserve unknown/default height and other actor attachments. Native line spacing and complete proxy anchors remain outside the verified support.

The official Bedrock 1.26.51.1/protocol 2193 Hive replay renders ordinary name glyphs opaquely over the dark foreground avatar: the Starter Bundle ROI has 462 pixels at RGB (84,255,84), and its following white row has 561 pixels at RGB (255,255,255). The target ordinary name material is unlit, so these saturated foreground values establish the default opaque pass without fitting a screenshot correction factor.

Java 26.3 submits a depth-tested opaque name pass and a see-through pass whose foreground alpha is max((backgroundOpacity + .75) * .5, .5). When an avatar occludes the opaque pass, only the dim overlay remains. Preserve foreground RGB, background color and packed light, but use opaque default glyph alpha for the ordinary native SEE_THROUGH name pass. Keep NORMAL/depth-tested passes and other connections unchanged.

The native actor-specific foreground RGBA getter is not fully traced. This change reproduces the captured default ordinary-name opacity; it does not claim support for every actor fade.

Recover the native clamped .005-block name height for proxy armor stands
whose explicit native zero width and height translate to Java marker state.
Bedrock 1.26.51.1 anchors names at scaled AABB height plus .7 blocks; Java
26.3 adds .5 after the attachment. Publish .005+.2 only for marker armor
stands in an accepted native rendering context. Ordinary Java connections,
unknown actors and ambiguous Interaction height one retain their fallback.

The marker flag carries proved empty-box semantics, not a fitted screen
offset. Native scale zero alone does not imply marker state. Direct native
snapshots remain authoritative and do not read mutable state while drawing.
Six focused anchor tests pass, including scoped marker recovery and the
unchanged sparse bounds, identity, fallback and lighting cases.

Bedrock 1.26.51.1/protocol 2193 name producer 141d55a60 selects
its depth-tested material and replaces foreground alpha with 0.125
from target constant 14e61e13c when actor flag 1 (sneaking) is set.
The ordinary name path retains actor foreground RGBA. Exact target PE
SHA-256 is 537c0aee2e79afbdc94b44b28e00f466ae62bc50e2733d953b430db9dbaa9ee7.

Java 26.3 SubmitNodeCollection.submitNameTag instead uses alpha
max((backgroundAlpha + 0.75) * 0.5, 0.5) for its discrete NORMAL pass.
Capture the enclosing seeThrough argument directly with MixinExtras
Local(argsOnly=true) and replace only the accepted native discrete
NORMAL foreground alpha with byte 32. Preserve RGB, background, light,
ordinary opaque NORMAL passes, other display modes, and Java sessions.
The accepted native ordinary SEE_THROUGH pass continues to use alpha 255.

This reproduces the verified crouching branch. It does not infer the
unmapped native internal flag 129, conditional PLAYER_INDEX color mode,
actor fade states, or glyph billboard transforms. Base actor and default
player foreground getters both return opaque white; they do not justify
a blanket gray player color.

Compilation and twelve focused nametag and anchor tests pass with no failures or skips.

Receive full skins through the core player_skin transport on direct and
ViaProxy connections. Feed completed records into the existing renderer.
Remove the direct-only configuration queue because core buffers initial
updates until Java play and capability registration. Capture the real
Minecraft connection in the per-listener receiver, so queued work from a
closed connection cannot enter its replacement session. Clear incomplete
transfers with the existing appearance lifecycle.

Use core's local-player ownership flag to install the received appearance
on the actual Minecraft avatar. The Java frontend UUID can differ from
the Bedrock actor UUID on a proxy connection. Keep remote identities intact.

Read native actor resources from accepted server packs on both connection
routes. Reuse core's bounded decoder and reconstructed resource storage,
including resolved stack order and original server provenance. Resource
reload and world cleanup release the cached graph snapshot. A missing or
invalid archive retains ordinary rendering without requesting Store login.
Custom actor state still requires its separate core transport through proxies.

Receive complete core-evaluated custom actor snapshots through the shared
negotiated channel on both direct connections and ViaProxy. Capture the
Minecraft connection at play initialization and install state on its
client thread. Replaced connections and cleared lifetimes cannot install
queued actor updates into the next session.

Remove the direct-only CustomEntity mixin. Core selects native transport
or converted Java item displays from the client's advertised capability,
so the add-on no longer cancels core fallback spawns globally. Empty model
updates hide actors without losing their identity; removals release them.
Semantic tests cover immutable scale updates, hidden selections, removal,
independent UUIDs, replacement and cleanup. Actor events, player properties
and complete native animation playback remain separate requirements.

Resolve transported local skins from the current play listener's profile,
which exists before the player entity. A proxy can use a Java UUID that
is different from the Bedrock account UUID. Do not install early local
skin fragments under the Bedrock UUID while waiting for that entity.

Share accepted native archive decoding between actor and effect consumers.
Cache layers and decode errors for the current resource manager until reload
or disconnect invalidates them. Select only the highest accepted server
resource, ignoring local files with the same identifier. Reading accepted
assets requires no Store sign-in and works through direct and ViaProxy routes.
Targeted tests cover both consumers, provenance, empty layers, resource-manager
replacement, explicit invalidation, ignored local packs and retry after reload.

Integrate native actor pipelines with Iris 1.11.7 shader selection through
an optional pseudo mixin. The macOS 26.3 client log and the pinned
Complementary Reimagined r5.9.3 replay show missing actor overrides.
Iris writes 56-byte entity vertices while the original GPU pipeline
still reads the vanilla 36-byte layout, corrupting positions.

Map ordinary and fullbright actors to the matching vanilla shader families
for Iris lookup only. Iris retains hand and shadow phase selection and
creates the replacement pipeline with its extended format. A shared
identity registry lets particle and cloud features supply their families
without loading Iris classes when the shader mod is absent. Preserve
native culling, depth, blending and vertex formats outside shader draws.
Semantic tests cover every actor material family and unrelated pipelines.
Iris source: https://github.com/IrisShaders/Iris/blob/26.3/common/src/main/java/net/irisshaders/iris/pipeline/IrisPipelines.java
Report: https://mclo.gs/er8RPm6

Use the native ten-pixel name row advance instead of Java font lineHeight.
Preserve the final row anchor and the component styles, blank rows and
Unicode codepoints. This applies only to accepted Bedrock name contexts.

Executing the original 1.26.51.1 build 51061372 name record routine confirms
ten pixels plus authored extra spacing. The ordinary and sneaking name
producer passes zero extra spacing and unit scale. Layout and anchor tests
cover twelve cases. Absolute glyph origin and native GPU comparisons remain
unverified; keep proprietary executable fixtures private.

Use the target name foreground consumer to determine pixel scale, row
origin and world lift. Preserve native float multiplication order and
integer half-width centering. Its original LF splitter drops only
zero-length rows; retain whitespace and translated segment styles.
Replace the earlier inferred final-row anchor with the executed consumer
layout. Ordinary Java names retain the original renderer path.

Original Bedrock 1.26.51.1 protocol 2193 instructions verify twelve
transform cases, 210 row/width cases, eight splitter controls and the empty
foreground exit. Font rasterization, camera/quaternion orientation,
backgrounds, stripped formatting-only rows and native GPU comparisons
remain separate requirements.

Sodium 0.9.2 caches box vertices and box UVs in the ModelPart.Cube
constructor, then replaces compile with that cached cuboid. Native
geometry installs authored polygons after construction, including zero-size
poly-mesh placeholders. Compile the current polygons in a native cube
subclass so per-face UVs, mesh topology and normals reach the actual
VertexConsumer with or without Sodium and Iris.

Keep unlit ordinary actors on Iris entity cutout programs instead of
glowing eyes. Complementary Reimagined r5.9.3 amplifies eye RGB for bloom,
which does not represent native ignore_lighting. Submit full-bright packed
light for lighting bypass without changing the native shader path. This
does not claim shader-pack parity for native inverse-alpha emissive masks.

The matching licensed material documents supply native inheritance.
Normalize built-in GPU layouts, sampler metadata and skinning variants
that Java replaces with fixed buffers and CPU poses. Keep server material
validation strict. Real bundled alpha-test families and authored polygon
submission have targeted regressions.

Return installed native player textures from PlayerInfo so tab and Chat
Heads consumers share the same skin. Show tab heads on offline Bedrock
connections without changing the connection authentication state.

When Iris replaces the native emissive shader, split inverse-alpha emission
into an opaque lit surface and additive radiance. The 1.26.51.1 entity
emissive shader weights lighting by alpha and radiance by inverse alpha;
Java transparency cannot represent that mask. Preserve UV transforms,
culling, upload generations and resource lifetimes for both passes.
The mask test checks every alpha/channel pair and radiance reconstruction.

Resolve native appearances in PlayerSkinRenderCache for inline player sprites and skull lookups, as well as the existing player-info path. Chat Heads 1.3.2-26.3 creates resolved PlayerSprite objects, whose glyph suppliers use this cache directly. Recheck retained suppliers against installed Bedrock appearances so late skin packets and replacements remain visible, preserve Java fallback for unknown identities, and bound the extra cache by weak profile keys. This bridges the public vanilla sprite API without changing Java UUIDs or depending on Chat Heads internals.

Provide a separate square head atlas for legacy 64x32 skins and their
scaled variants. Java 26.3 PlayerGlyphProvider.Instance samples face UVs
at 8,8 and hat UVs at 40,8 against a 64x64 layout. Supplying the original
rectangular texture therefore samples half the correct vertical offset.
Preserve every native pixel and pad the lower half with transparent pixels.
Keep the original body texture for native geometry. Release both textures
when an appearance is replaced or cleared. Square and custom atlas images
retain their existing identity. Pixel tests cover three legacy resolutions,
face and hat UVs, transparent padding, and unchanged square images.

Captured Galaxite on Bedrock 1.26.51.1/protocol 2193 supplies 128x128
images whose 128x128 geometry keeps eight-unit head cube UVs. Other
skins use a 64x64 geometry with the same image size. Java glyphs always
sample a canonical 64-unit atlas, so texture resolution alone cannot
select their face region. Project authored head and hat UVs using the
model texture dimensions onto a private Java head atlas. Preserve all
six faces for skull consumers, source alpha, and the untouched native
body texture. Ordinary canonical skins continue sharing their texture.
Flatten only ordinary unrotated eight-unit head cubes; arbitrary mesh
portraits and animated persona faces remain separate rendering work.
Pixel tests cover both geometry dimensions on the same double-size image.

Retain the difference between native and Java vehicle yaw when applying rider anchors. Boat Java yaw is offset by ninety degrees, but the native rider seat remains defined in native vehicle coordinates. Directional boat anchor tests cover quarter and half turns.

Reuse resolved actor draws while the immutable model snapshot, accepted resources and Nether lighting remain unchanged. Bound retained snapshots to 1,024 actor identities. Clear the cache on resource or world replacement, while poses, UV animations, scale and yaw continue their existing frame evaluation. Targeted tests cover identity invalidation, failed resolution retries and least-recently-used eviction.

Treat a parsed JSON null skin geometry value as absent, including ordinary
JSON whitespace. The captured Enchanted scene supplies three standard skins
with null followed by a newline. Preserve their existing standard geometry
path without a spurious parse warning; malformed non-null payloads still
report a failure.

Co-authored-by: Miles Tech Studios <291174896+milesalexg-tech@users.noreply.github.com>

Carry the native ordinary actor alpha reference through Iris's compiled
pipeline override and bind scope. Iris 1.11.7 shares ExtendedShader and
FallbackShader programs between native and Java pipelines; mutating the
program or its ShaderKey leaks the cutoff into unrelated draws. Restore
the prior thread scope even if a bind throws, and restore each fallback
program's original uniform on ordinary Java binds.

Bedrock 1.26.51.1/protocol 2193 ordinary Actor AlphaTest retains sampled
texture alpha strictly above 0.5. The owning native shader evidence also
applies to supplied player bodies. Iris's entity cutout family uses 0.1.
Actual GL uniform readbacks verify native 0.5 and Java 0.1 in the same
shared programs. This carries the reference only: shader-pack-authored
comparison functions and post-shader alpha differ from the native
source-texture test and still require separate parity work.

Keep ordinary native actor coverage independent of shader-pack output
alpha and Iris alpha-function overrides. Carry the original UV attribute
through ordinary vertex/fragment programs, test the original unit-zero
texture with the native strict comparison, and retain Iris's generated
comparison only for Java draws. Bind native selection and reference after
all Iris uniform updates, including when a program is shared with Java.
Register a separate external sampler so gtexture replacement cannot change
the native source. Apply the same coverage contract to synthesized fallback
programs without rewriting shader-pack authored discards.

Use Iris 1.11.7 and its GLSL AST API as compile-only dependencies. Iris is
still optional and none of its classes or parser libraries are embedded.
Geometry and tessellation programs retain the existing reference bridge
until their varying transport is implemented and verified. This change
does not establish arbitrary shader-pack visual parity.

Resolve opaque and alpha-tested ENTITY materials through one effective
material value instead of treating every accepted family as alpha-tested.
Carry coverage through actor draws, dimension lighting and the Iris
emissive split. Opaque texels retain native color and depth at zero alpha;
removing ALPHA_TEST from an inherited material no longer selects Java
translucent rendering.

The pinned Bedrock 1.26.51.1 Entity.material.bin (SHA-256
403efcc7777c9712176f6715f861c873e31d8e45d0b73b8e320bd29754789058)
contains BaseColor and Emissive passes beside AlphaTest and
AlphaTestEmissive. Their sampled SM65 vertex shaders are byte-identical.
The opaque fragments retain the same lighting, overlay, inverse-alpha
emission and fog arithmetic, without either discard operation. Unmodified
Lazurite 0.10 reads the complete version-26 container through its closing
magic and end of file. Original fragment IR controls cover all four
families; supplied texture, interpolation, constant-buffer and output
boundaries remain explicit. Native rasterization and complete Iris
shader-pack output parity remain separate requirements.

Pair shader-pack emission with the complete geometry-producing stages of
its exact depth-writing native body. Separate entity and eye programs can
apply different vertex deformation: the inspected Iris 1.11.7 output for
Complementary Reimagined 5.9.3 offsets chicken depth only in the body,
which hides the additive eye surface. Preserve the emission fragment and
framebuffer directives, body attribute decisions and all geometry/tessellation
stages instead of hardcoding a pack name, entity ID or depth correction.

Scope each lookup to the native pipeline being compiled, including nested
compilation and cached overrides. Cache paired programs by both body and
emission keys in their current Iris world pipeline; its normal loaded-shader
lifetime closes them on reload. Retain body lightmap and overlay bindings.
Unsupported stage interfaces keep the prior eye path with one diagnostic
per selection. Ordinary pipelines, GUI portraits and shadow selection stay
outside this pairing. This does not establish arbitrary shader-pack parity,
complete hand behavior, emissive overlay order or framebuffer alpha.

Resolve inherited depthFunc declarations before material admission. The
pinned native villager material explicitly selects LessEqual, which the
existing actor pipeline already implements with Java's reversed-depth
GREATER_THAN_OR_EQUAL comparison. Rejecting that declaration prevented
standard villager graphs from drawing after their query inputs resolved.

Accept the implemented comparison when declared explicitly or inherited.
Keep unsupported comparisons distinct so a child can restore LessEqual
without losing valid parent shader settings. Reject malformed string
fields rather than coercing numeric, array or null values. Other depth
comparisons and masked multitexture materials retain their fallback.

Receive negotiated custom-actor sampler snapshots on the version-two
channel while retaining the original receiver for older core versions.
Keep ordered immutable bindings in the existing connection-owned render
store so secondary texture changes invalidate its model cache. Ordinary
single-sampler rendering explicitly uses slot zero; additional slots no
longer arrive as duplicate geometry. Masked material drawing remains a
separate shared renderer integration and is not enabled by transport.

Carry the verified masked-overwrite material mode through native actor
pipelines. Bind independent color and mask textures in one draw, preserve
their own sampling grids, and apply the target ceil/clamp selector before
alpha testing, overlay, lighting and fog. Keep Minecraft's overlay sampler
separate from the Bedrock mask resource. Accept the verified alpha-test
masked family and continue rejecting unverified emission combinations.

The target 1.26.51.1 sampled SM65 fragment uses ceil of mask RGB sum times
inverse alpha to choose between complete source texels. The GPU regression
compares actual production GLSL RGBA, discard and depth with 512 original
fragment controls using supplied sampling/buffer boundaries. Constant and
shared-input variants and native sampling/filter producers remain outside
that comparison. All material consumers and Iris require matching bindings.

Compose masked color inputs before shader-pack shading and test native
coverage from the original UV. Keep the mask on its own reserved Iris
texture unit and preserve the overlay/lightmap bindings. Forward sampler
identity through GLSL helper parameters, including overloads and prototypes,
so normal-map and other sampler reads remain unchanged. Native mask state
belongs to each pipeline bind and restores with nested Java draws.

The Iris bridge handles normalized texture, LOD and gradient reads in the
supported vertex/fragment stages. Geometry/tessellation transport and
other shader-pack sampling operations still need separate verification.
Retain the legacy entity fallback for older snapshots missing sampler data.

Gate Iris integration by the installed mod, not only target-class presence.
Its binding hook targets a method Iris adds to Minecraft's GlProgram, so
an absent Iris must skip that mixin while an installed Iris keeps strict
injection validation. The fresh plain-client startup exposed this path.
